<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tabora</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tabora is a city in west-central Tanzania, in the land of the Nyamwezi. It grew from the 1840s around a settlement of Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili merchants</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of Unyanyembe, becoming the chief inland depot of the caravan trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves between the coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. Explorers such as Burton, Speke, Livingstone and Stanley passed through, and under German rule it became an administrative centre and a station on the Central Railway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tabora.jpg" alt="Tabora" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wesellgold entertainment · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Around_Kanyenye_Street_,Tabora_Manicipality,_Tanzania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tabora is the capital of Tanzania's Tabora Region and the administrative seat of Tabora Urban District, which had a population of 226,999 at the 2012 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It has a tropical savanna climate with two seasons of about equal length, a wet season from November to April and a dry season from May to October. Its people rely mainly on agriculture, as subsistence farmers or small-scale tobacco growers. The streets are lined with century-old mango trees planted by Omani traders, and the town is known as the fruit capital of western Tanzania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Coastal traders settled in the region to take advantage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slave caravan trade, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> and Omani traders established Kazeh near present-day Tabora. By 1870 the town had between 5,000 and 10,000 inhabitants living in roughly fifty large square houses. Each held up to several hundred people and had inner courtyards, garden plots, store rooms, servant quarters and outbuildings for slaves. Nyamwezi villages surrounded it, and their people provided produce and caravan labour. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar appointed a representative there, the town was part of the Kingdom of Unyanyembe, and traders came from as far north as the Kingdom of Buganda. In August 1871 the forces of the Nyamwezi ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirambo">Mirambo</a> sacked it and burned a quarter of the town.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> was proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the region in 1885, but as late as 1891 travellers reported Tabora to be a lawless town, and the German administration gained control of it only later that year. As a major station on the Central Line it became the most important administrative centre of central German East Africa. In 1916 the colonial garrison struck an emergency coinage there, mostly crude <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and brass rupie coins marked T. During the Tabora Offensive of the East African Campaign, Belgian Congo colonial forces, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/force-publique">Force Publique</a> under General Charles Tombeur, captured the town on 19 September 1916 after ten days and nights of heavy fighting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tabora is a junction on the Central railway line, which runs east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>, west to Kigoma on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a> and north to Mwanza on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>. Roads lead to Singida, Kigoma, Mbeya and Mwanza, though salt flats to the west prevent a direct road to Kigoma. The town is also known for two schools. Tabora Boys Secondary School was founded in 1922 to educate the sons of African chiefs and was called the Eton of Tanganyika, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> studied there. Tabora Girls Secondary School was established in 1928.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tabora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Central Railroad, 1,247 kilometres long, linked Dar es Salaam with Morogoro, <b>Tabora</b> and Kigoma, and harbours were fitted with electric cranes, rail access and warehouses; in 1912 Dar es Salaam and Tanga received 356 steamers and over 1,000 coastal vessels.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Caravans organised by Arab, Swahili and Nyamwezi traders and financed by Indian merchants travelled inland to <b>Tabora</b>, Ujiji on Lake Tanganyika and beyond, returning with ivory and captives; Tippu Tip, the most famous of these traders, built a personal domain in the eastern Congo basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From his capital at Iselamagazi he conquered neighbouring chiefdoms and created a large state known as Urambo, west of <b>Tabora</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mirambos-state">Mirambo's state</a></li><li><blockquote>Nyungu ya Mawe, a member of the ruling family of Unyanyembe, broke with his kin and the Arab traders of <b>Tabora</b> in the 1860s.</blockquote><a href="/state/nyungu-ya-mawes-state">Nyungu ya Mawe's state</a></li><li><blockquote>From the early 19th century Nyamwezi porters and traders opened caravan routes to the coast, and in the 1840s Arab and Swahili merchants settled at <b>Tabora</b>, which grew into a commercial town under the chief Fundikira.</blockquote><a href="/state/unyanyembe">Unyanyembe</a></li></ul></section>
